---
// src/pages/projects/example-project.astro
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Medical CRM — Nicholas Grzelak">
  <article class="project-page">
    <header class="project-header">
      <p class="label">PB-004 · Personal Build</p>
      <h1>Medical CRM</h1>
      <p class="project-lede">
        Customer management tool for medical clinics. Used to log patients, write notes, record purchases and categorize followups
      </p>
      <p class="label project-facts">
        2023 · Python · UI/UX
      </p>
    </header>

    <!--
      Images: drop files into the /public folder of your project, e.g.
      public/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg, then reference them from the
      site root like below. Delete this placeholder block once you have
      real images.
    -->
    <figure class="project-figure">
      <!-- <div class="img-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
        RENDER — ISO VIEW (replace with your image)
      </div> -->
        <img src="/projects/crm/CRMlogin.jpg" alt="CRM login page" />
      <!-- <img src="/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg" alt="Isometric CAD render of the plotter frame" /> -->
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 1 — CRM login page</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The basic functionality of this app was for a clinic to track and manage their patients. A user would sign in, and be able to add a patient that they 
      have just seen, and then in time of their choosing they could have a reminder to follow up with the patient. Not only does it make it easier for clinicians 
      to keep track of followups all the information and patient notes would be accessible to them. The app would also record sales allowing invoicing and reciepts to be
      managed.
    </p>

    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      I tried to make it a sleek design, I took some inspiration from other CRMs like Salesforce. 
      The app was made you Dash in python and bootstrap was used to give it a modern UI.
      The hashing and encryption was done with the cryptograph package.
      The encryption keys were stored with a password protect in a pickle file. 
      SQL was used to manage client information and do checks.
      The app had the capability to import clients and pricing schemes through a .csv file.
    </p>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <!-- <div class="img-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
        RENDER — ISO VIEW (replace with your image)
      </div> -->
        <img src="/projects/crm/CRMclient.jpg" alt="CRM Client Addition" />
      <!-- <img src="/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg" alt="Isometric CAD render of the plotter frame" /> -->
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 2 — CRM Client Addition</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      The project taught me the basics of security by using a user encryption key to hide senstive data and hashing to store passwords. 
      This was one of the first big projects that I used github for, I tracked issues and maintained version controls.
      I also improved on my UI/UX design skills by building a python app for a customer and got feedback on the design. I tried
      to deploy the app onto AWS but was not successful and in the days before large LLM models troubleshooting was a large task.
      Unfortunately the project was never used and abandoned during my undergrad
    </p>

    <p class="back-link"><a href="/">← Back to index</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .project-page {
    padding: 4rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .project-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .project-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .project-lede {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 52ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .project-facts {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .project-page h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .project-figure {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .project-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .img-placeholder {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-align: center;
    padding: 1rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
</style>